title: Urban Heat Island (UHI) Analyzer
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the
  metadata from this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Suyog
    family-names: Gautam
    name-particle: Suyog
    email: suyoggtm@gmail.com
    affiliation: Virginia Tech
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0008-1831-0601'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/suyogautam/Global-UHI-Analyzer-'
abstract: >-
  Urban heat islands (UHIs) — where city surfaces are
  significantly warmer than surrounding rural areas — are a
  well-documented consequence of urbanization, yet
  accessible tools for systematic long-term UHI monitoring
  remain limited. This study presents an open-source,
  web-based UHI analysis platform built on Google Earth
  Engine and Streamlit, enabling satellite-derived thermal
  and land surface analysis for any user-defined area of
  interest without requiring local computing resources or
  programming expertise.

  The platform integrates two complementary satellite
  pipelines: Landsat Collection 2 Level-2 (Landsat 5/7/8/9,
  30 m spatial resolution, 2000–present) for fine-scale US
  analysis, and MODIS Terra/Aqua merged products
  (MOD11A1/MYD11A1, ~1 km, 2000–present) for global coverage
  including nighttime land surface temperature. For each
  year in a user-defined period, the app automatically
  identifies the peak thermal month, applies
  quality-controlled compositing, and derives land surface
  temperature (LST), NDVI, NDMI, and NDBI from
  atmospherically corrected surface reflectance. Urban and
  vegetated reference zones are delineated using either the
  National Land Cover Database (NLCD, 30 m, US-only) or
  MODIS MCD12Q1 IGBP land cover (500 m, global), with
  cropland and water excluded from the vegetated reference
  to reduce phenological noise. UHI intensity is computed as
  the mean LST difference between urban and vegetated
  pixels. Temporal trends are assessed using Sen's slope
  estimator and the Mann–Kendall non-parametric significance
  test applied to both annual values and 3-year centered
  moving averages. Satellite-derived LST anomalies are
  independently validated against ERA5-Land 2 m air
  temperature reanalysis via Pearson correlation. Outputs
  include interactive multi-layer maps, downloadable results
  tables, trend charts, GeoTIFF rasters, and shapefiles with
  per-year attributes.

  The tool is designed for researchers, urban planners, and
  climate practitioners seeking reproducible, multi-decadal
  UHI assessments across diverse geographic contexts and
  spatial scales.
